WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.409 --> 00:00:08.590
Google was founded on September 4, 1998, and
for most of that time Google.com has been

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.080
the most trafficked website in the world.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:16.570
Although it has faced stiff competition from
Facebook in recent years, it still remains

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:25.470
the #1 most visited website.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.779
The amount of searches Google processes every
day is mind boggling.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:38.950
In 2012 people made a total of 1,873,910,000,000
google searches.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:49.309
That's an average of 5,134,000,000 searches
per day or 59,421 searches per second.

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:53.590
Yet Google manages to return every single
one of these search results in about 300 milliseconds.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:54.690
But the infrastructure required to support
all this is enormous, and unfathomably expensive.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:55.690
Google are highly secretive about the number
and locations of their data centres.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:56.690
But it is estimated they have over 2 million
servers worldwide.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.899
And Google spends over $4 billion on new data
centres and servers every year.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:07.160
It's easy to think that not much happens when
you type something into Google, but just two

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:12.440
google searches produce the same amount of
carbon dioxide as boiling a kettle.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.860
But Google isn't just a search engine.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:21.110
Google provides over 100 products and services
including Google Mail, Drive, Plus, Translate,

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:25.310
Maps, Play, Analytics, Blogger and many, many
more.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:33.670
Oh and don't forget YouTube, which Google
bought for a measly $1.65 billion in 2006.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.830
Commonly searched terms are cached.

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:40.940
This means Google doesn't have to recalculate
the results every time it is searched and

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.520
the cached search results can be served up
almost instantaneously to the user.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:50.619
However, every day 15% of searches made have
never before been typed into the search engine.

00:01:50.619 --> 00:01:56.869
That's 500 million searches every day that
Google has never seen before, and it has always

00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:00.890
been that way for the 15 years the company
has existed.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.090
Surprisingly the percentage of brand new searches
made on Google has never really dropped.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:10.060
Brand new searches like this take up a lot
more processing power and take considerably

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.360
longer to render the results, because everything
has to be calculated from scratch.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.460
So to combat this, Google recently added a
new product to its search, called Knowledge

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.460
Graph.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:26.360
Knowledge Graph is a vast database which holds
copious amounts of data on a wide range of

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:32.390
entities, such as topics, people and events,
the system also observes the connections between

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.500
them, similar to the human brain.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.520
This allows Google to present you with answers
instead of just a list of links.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.320
You can see this in action, by asking Google
questions such as "What is the speed of light?".

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.380
But if Google is so advanced, why is the homepage
so basic?

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.290
The main reason it's so bare is because the
Google founders, Larry Page and Sergey Brin,

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.340
didn't know HTML and just wanted a quick interface.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.569
In fact for a long time there was no submit
button and the only way to kick start your

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:04.520
google search was by pressing the return key.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:09.290
Due to the sparseness of the homepage in early
user tests they noticed people were just sitting

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:10.700
around looking at the screen.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:14.580
After minutes of nothingness, the tester asked
"What's up?".

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.910
To which they replied "We are waiting for
the rest of it".

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:22.430
To solve this problem, the Google Copyright
message was added to act as a crude end of

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:23.430
page marker.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.340
But how did the name Google come about?

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.830
Well the name was actually an accident.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:33.459
The founders were going for the name Googol,
which is a one followed by one hundred zeros.

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:36.270
However they misspelt it as Google.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.570
The I'm feeling lucky button, which takes
users straight to the first search result

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:42.560
is nearly never used.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.160
However in trials, they found removing it
somehow reduces the Google experience.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:54.310
It is estimated to cost Google around $100
million in lost advertising revenue every

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:55.310
year.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:04:00.870
One of Google's most successful features was
the "Did you mean..." spelling corrector.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.250
When implemented this instantly doubled their
traffic.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:08.110
Google has the largest network or translators
in the world.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:12.720
Google is constantly developing new features,
but before they can be released to the entire

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:19.000
Google user base, Google tests them on a small
percentage and closely monitors the results.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.100
At least 20% of people have to use a feature
or it will be scrapped.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:28.730
Unlike many large corporations, emailing Google
isn't fruitless.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.130
Google actively listens to feedback.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:37.520
Speaking of email, Gmail was used internally
for 2 years before it's launch to the public.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:44.070
Until recently all Google employees were encouraged
to spend 20% of their time at work, working

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.870
on their own projects.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.840
This innovative management technique gave
rise to some of Google's biggest projects,

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:56.840
including AdSense, Gmail, Google Transit,
Google News and Google Talk.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:03.780
Some core Google services recently went down
for 5 minutes causing worldwide internet traffic

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:07.699
to drop off by an enormous 40 per cent.

00:05:07.699 --> 00:05:13.110
New Google employees are called "Noogles",
they are initiated by having to wear a propeller

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.710
beanie cap on their first Friday.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:21.760
Google loves food, the first chef that Google
employed earned $26 million from his stock

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:22.760
options.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:30.139
Google's headquarters, aptly named The Googleplex
has 18 cafeterias on campus, with diverse

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:31.300
menus.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:39.139
What's more, all Google employees are provided
with free, restaurant quality breakfast, lunch

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:40.670
and dinner, including drinks.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:45.880
But working at Google comes with an array
of free benefits, including on-site gyms and

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:52.610
daycare as well as a complementary concierge
service to assist with everyday tasks.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:58.620
The Googleplex is also fitted out with laundry
rooms, two swimming pools and multiple sand

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.470
volleyball courts.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:06.770
As well as some bizarre installations like
a gigantic T-Rex skeleton, a scale replica

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.100
of SpaceShipOne, enormous Android-themed models,
pink flamingos, a large LEGO man, Google-coloured

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.180
phone boxes and adult sized ball pits.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:21.790
Google employees can give their co-workers
massage credits for a job well done on a project.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:25.810
These credits can be redeemed for a free one
hour massage on campus.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:31.280
Every friday employees can unwind and socialise
with free beer and wine.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.390
Google employees receive incredible death
benefits.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:39.210
If a Googler passes away, their surviving
spouse continues to get half of their salary

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:45.400
for the next 10 years as well as $1,000 a
month for each of the Googler's children.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:50.020
And finally, Google's first ever Tweet in
February 2009 looked like this:

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.009
"I'm 01100110 01100101 01100101 01101100 01101001
01101110 01100111 00100000 01101100 01110101

00:06:52.009 --> 00:06:53.009
01100011 01101011 01111001 00001010."

00:06:53.009 --> 00:07:00.090
Which, when translated from binary code reads
"I'm feeling lucky".

